      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The widest transverse dimension of the adult female birth canal (pelvic outlet) under maximal physiological stretch is roughly 120–130 mm, while the obstetric conjugate (narrowest bony inlet) is ≈110 mm.",
      "evidence": "Obstetrics textbooks and clinical measurements of pelvic dimensions (Cunningham et al., 2014; studies of pelvic outlet expansion during second stage labor).",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The skull’s smallest diameter (140 mm) is larger than the largest achievable transverse diameter of the birth canal (≈130 mm) by at least 10 mm.",
      "evidence": "Direct numerical comparison of steps 1 and 2.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Fully fused adult cranial bone is rigid; compressive strain beyond ~2 % causes cortical fracture, so reducing a 140 mm diameter by ≥10 mm (~7 %) would fracture the skull.",
      "evidence": "Biomechanical properties of cortical bone and fracture thresholds reported in orthopedic literature.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the skull is both larger than the maximal pelvic opening and cannot be deformed enough without breaking, an intact adult skull cannot pass through the birth canal.",
      "evidence": "Logical synthesis of steps 3 and 4.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
  ],
